{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.7
    ,
      "why": "Only family I own that can plausibly reach the burst rate. The system constraint (32 results within 0.5 us of a 1 MHz tick) demands ~64 Msps of burst service, i.e. cycles-per-result C = ceil(N/k)+3 must be small while Fmax stays high. Accuracy (max_abs_err <= 2^-12) pins N to roughly 13-16 and W to roughly 14-16, so C is minimised by pushing k up (k=8 gives C=5 at N=13-16). The k sweep 2..8 is exactly the C-vs-Fmax trade-off the front needs: large k lowers C but lengthens the combinational chain, small k does the reverse. angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding are swept because they buy accuracy bits cheaply and let the search trade area for error near the 2^-12 boundary."
    },
    {
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.3
    ,
      "why": "Area baseline and the cheapest possible datapath (one shared micro-rotation, barrel shifters). C = N+3 >= 15 at the N needed for 2^-12, so it almost certainly cannot satisfy sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5 us, but it is the minimum-area anchor of the accuracy/area curve and the reference point for the fleet's merged front. Keeping it in the search is cheap insurance in case the L1 batch bound is looser than the serial-service model suggests, and it gives the hypervolume a low-area end if any iterative point is feasible."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Two constraints dominate the geometry. (1) Accuracy: max_abs_err <= 2^-12 needs N >= ~13 micro-rotations and W >= ~14 (output LSB 2^-(W-2)), so the useful search box is N 12..20, W 12..18, with angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding as the fine-tuning knobs. (2) The system scenario: a 1 MHz tick dumps 32 requests at once and all must land within 0.5 us, i.e. ~64 Msps burst service, twice the 32 Msps average constraint. For serial families this means cycles-per-result C = ceil(N/k)+3 (unrolled_k) or N+3 (iterative) must be small at high Fmax. iterative is essentially out (C>=15 needs ~1 GHz); unrolled_k is the only family I own with a shot, and only at large k. So the budget is weighted 70/30 toward unrolled_k, sweeping k across its full 2..8 range to expose the C-vs-Fmax trade-off, while iterative anchors the low-area end. I am not pre-empting the infeasibility decision: code computes Fmax, area and the L1 batch bound, and if no point clears all three constraints the correct outcome is `infeasible` rather than a violating design."
}

{
  "decision": "add_family",
  "rationale": "Both explored families are structurally unable to reach the throughput constraint: iterative and unrolled_k issue one result every several cycles, and their best observed throughput is 9.47 MSPS and 8.43 MSPS respectively, i.e. 100% of evaluations violate throughput_msps >= 32. The system constraint sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5 is also violated by ~700-940% because 32 back-to-back requests cannot be drained at <10 MSPS. The registry contains two families not yet tried, pipelined and pipelined_m, which produce 1 result/cycle (throughput = Fmax) and have short latency (N+2 or ceil(N/m)+2 cycles). Those are the only families whose structure can plausibly satisfy both the 32 MSPS throughput and the 0.5 us batch bound, so the next round should bring them in.",
  "next_plan": {
    "families": [
      {
        "family": "pipelined",
        "ranges": [
          {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
          {"param": "n_iter", "low": 8, "high": 16, "choices": null},
          {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
          {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
          {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
        ]
      ,
      "budget_share": 0.5
      ,
      "why": "1 result/cycle; latency N+2 cycles. Need W>=14 for 2^-12 accuracy, but keep 12..20 to let the search find the area/accuracy trade-off. n_iter 8..16 keeps latency low enough for the 0.5 us batch bound while allowing enough rotations for accuracy."
      },
      {
        "family": "pipelined_m",
        "ranges": [
          {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
          {"param": "n_iter", "low": 8, "high": 16, "choices": null},
          {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
          {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
          {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
          {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
        ]
      ,
      "budget_share": 0.5
      ,
      "why": "1 result/cycle with fewer pipeline registers than full pipelined; latency ceil(N/m)+2 cycles. m 2..8 trades area/FFs against Fmax and latency, which is exactly the region needed for the 0.5 us batch bound."
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "Focus the remaining 40 evaluations on the two 1-result/cycle families. Keep data_width and n_iter ranges wide enough to expose the area/accuracy Pareto front, but cap n_iter at 16 so latency does not dominate the 0.5 us batch budget. If the L1 system bound is met, the L2 simulation will later re-check the cheapest designs."
  }
}
